Pushing the Packer files started a build in each repo. Nothing is live-changed until a build's rolling update finishes. Run this in Cloud Shell about 10 minutes from now. It's read-only:

```bash
P=pg-ae-n-app-125609
date -u
gcloud builds list --region=me-central2 --project=$P --limit=4 --format="table(createTime,status,substitutions.TRIGGER_NAME,substitutions.SHORT_SHA)"
echo "== KEY LINES FROM BUILD LOGS =="
gcloud logging read "logName=\"projects/$P/logs/cloudbuild\" AND (textPayload:\"upgraded,\" OR textPayload:\"APP IS UP\" OR textPayload:\"APACHE PATH OK\" OR textPayload:\"FATAL\" OR textPayload:\"matched pipeline templates\" OR textPayload:\"DRY RUN would delete\" OR textPayload:\"SKIP in use\" OR textPayload:\"Group is stable\")" --project=$P --freshness=2h --limit=40 --format="value(timestamp,resource.labels.build_id,textPayload)" | cut -c1-200 | sort
echo "== GROUPS =="
gcloud compute instance-groups managed list --project=$P --format="table(name,size,targetSize,instanceTemplate.basename())"
```
Both builds ran after `_Default` was re-enabled, so their logs are readable now.

## What success looks like

| Check | Expected |
|---|---|
| Builds | both `SUCCESS` for the new commits. A rollout can take 15 to 20 minutes, so `WORKING` means wait |
| `upgraded,` line | an apt summary such as `26 upgraded, …`, showing the build patched the image |
| `APP IS UP` and, for the frontend, `APACHE PATH OK` | as in earlier builds |
| `Group is stable` | the rolling update finished |
| Frontend cleanup | `matched pipeline templates: 8`, and three dry-run lines: `13633c6`, `7696009` and `b0ad954`, each with its image. They're the three oldest beyond `KEEP=5`. This is the first readable proof that the filter fix works |
| Backend cleanup | `matched pipeline templates: 5`, and no deletion lines, because 5 equals the keep count |
| Groups | both at size 1, with the new template names |

Before you ever set `DRY_RUN=0`, check the names in those lines.

## If a build fails

Rolling updates replace servers only after a successful build, so a failure changes nothing live. Send me the build id and:
```bash
gcloud builds describe BUILD_ID --region=me-central2 --project=$P --format="yaml(status,failureInfo,steps[].id,steps[].status)"
```
The likeliest cause is the new `apt-get upgrade` restarting SSH and dropping Packer's connection. I expect a restart to be deferred, but I haven't proven it. Revert that commit to restore the old behavior.

## The real test comes about an hour after the new servers boot

Both servers' upgrade timers fire 25 to 60 minutes after boot. Check them then, from the backend server (it's inside the VPC, and Cloud Shell isn't):
```bash
P=pg-ae-n-app-125609
for S in backend frontend; do
  N=$(gcloud compute instances list --project=$P --filter="name~cn-$S-mig" --format="value(name)" --limit=1)
  Z=$(gcloud compute instances list --project=$P --filter="name~cn-$S-mig" --format="value(zone.basename())" --limit=1)
  echo "== $N"
  gcloud compute ssh $N --zone=$Z --project=$P --internal-ip --command="uptime; sudo tail -n 4 /var/log/unattended-upgrades/unattended-upgrades.log | cut -c1-170"
done
gcloud compute instance-groups managed list --project=$P --format="table(name,size,targetSize)"
```
If gcloud says reauthentication is needed, run `gcloud auth login --no-launch-browser` first. Success means the log shows no packages, or only a few, and both groups stay at size 1. A server that scales out at that time would show the fix didn't work, and I'd look at CPU again before changing anything else. A server whose uptime is under about 25 minutes may not have hit its timer yet, so wait.

Don't push anything else until both groups are back at 1 after the rollout.

